00:14:51,020 --> 00:14:52,260
No one's gonna listen to that.

248
00:14:52,260 --> 00:14:54,460
Okay, sure, but at the same time,

249
00:14:54,460 --> 00:14:57,140
that hyper focus on the things that are wrong

250
00:14:57,140 --> 00:14:59,220
and the things that are weird

251
00:14:59,220 --> 00:15:03,420
is the reason why so many of us end up in a doom scroll.

252
00:15:03,420 --> 00:15:06,100
Like what good is that doing us?

253
00:15:06,100 --> 00:15:08,140
I think a lot of people are familiar

254
00:15:08,140 --> 00:15:11,300
with the experience of reading or listening to

255
00:15:11,300 --> 00:15:15,460
watching the news and then feeling a lot worse afterwards.

256
00:15:15,460 --> 00:15:19,700
There's plenty of boys and out there, not a lot of antidote.

257
00:15:19,700 --> 00:15:22,020
Another person that I talked to said,

258
00:15:22,020 --> 00:15:24,660
the primary ethos of journalism is,

259
00:15:24,660 --> 00:15:27,100
everything is a mess, good luck to you.

260
00:15:27,100 --> 00:15:35,100
What is the point of news that we can't do anything about

261
00:15:35,100 --> 00:15:36,700
or do anything with?

262
00:15:36,700 --> 00:15:38,700
Plain crashes.

263
00:15:38,700 --> 00:15:43,340
Well, that's sad and scary, kind of makes me want to stay home.

264
00:15:43,340 --> 00:15:48,020
But a story about how air traffic control actually works and, you know, 10 things you'll

265
00:15:48,020 --> 00:15:50,620
see on the tarmac that are keeping you safe.

266
00:15:50,620 --> 00:15:53,740
Now I have information with a purpose.

267
00:15:53,740 --> 00:15:59,940
I feel better at the airport, less confused when, you know, my plane is taxiing for 40

268
00:15:59,940 --> 00:16:00,940
minutes.

269
00:16:00,940 --> 00:16:05,180
I might be more patient, more empathetic to the people who do the job.

270
00:16:05,180 --> 00:16:12,100
I might care more about who in Congress wants to privatize air traffic control and how air

271
00:16:12,100 --> 00:16:14,840
traffic controllers feel about that.

272
00:16:14,840 --> 00:16:19,420
And the result is you're less anxious on a plane and you have a new piece of information

273
00:16:19,420 --> 00:16:21,660
for the next time you vote for your congressperson.

274
00:16:21,660 --> 00:16:24,180
Sure, yeah, exactly.

275
00:16:24,180 --> 00:16:28,980
And Nick, this story doesn't get my attention because something is weird or wrong.

276
00:16:28,980 --> 00:16:33,700
It gets my attention because there is something in there that I find useful.

277
00:16:33,700 --> 00:16:37,900
Tom has found news outlets that are trying this tactic,

278
00:16:37,900 --> 00:16:40,960
focusing on something called user needs,

279
00:16:40,960 --> 00:16:44,740
or the job to be done when it comes to the kinds of stories

280
00:16:44,740 --> 00:16:45,580
they're telling.

281
00:16:45,580 --> 00:16:50,580
The concept of the job to be done goes to this question of,

282
00:16:50,900 --> 00:16:52,500
is it what I think is important,

283
00:16:52,500 --> 00:16:55,220
or is it what the audience thinks is important?

284
00:16:55,220 --> 00:16:58,100
The guy who developed this concept

285
00:16:58,100 --> 00:17:00,700
was a disruption theorist at Harvard Business School

286
00:17:00,700 --> 00:17:02,500
named Clayton Christensen.

287
00:17:02,500 --> 00:17:10,100
And he realized that the job to be done is often not what the producer of the product thinks it is.

288
00:17:10,100 --> 00:17:17,180
Tom says that once a newsroom starts thinking about what people actually need, what they will actually use,

289
00:17:17,180 --> 00:17:21,500
more people might turn to that newsroom for exactly that.

290
00:17:21,500 --> 00:17:27,700
Like he talked about this one particular arm of the BBC, which started identifying user needs.

291
00:17:27,700 --> 00:17:31,420
Stuff readers and listeners could actually use.

292
00:17:31,420 --> 00:17:34,980
They made labels, one was update me.

293
00:17:34,980 --> 00:17:38,200
Well, that just sounds like the news to me, like hard news.

294
00:17:38,200 --> 00:17:41,020
These are more like threads of notifications

295
00:17:41,020 --> 00:17:42,860
and they're really common.

296
00:17:42,860 --> 00:17:45,620
The president plans to meet a world leader

297
00:17:45,620 --> 00:17:48,440
and then the update is the president is meeting

298
00:17:48,440 --> 00:17:49,580
with the world leader.

299
00:17:49,580 --> 00:17:51,700
And then it's like, here's what the president said

300
00:17:51,700 --> 00:17:52,660
to the world leader.

301
00:17:52,660 --> 00:17:55,420
Right, and then here's what the world leader said

302
00:17:55,420 --> 00:17:57,060
after the meeting.

303
00:17:57,060 --> 00:17:59,860
They're developments adding layers of information

304
00:17:59,860 --> 00:18:01,900
as more stuff happens.

305
00:18:01,900 --> 00:18:05,800
So when the BBC started labeling their stories.

306
00:18:05,800 --> 00:18:10,380
At the beginning, they noticed that 70% of the stories

307
00:18:10,380 --> 00:18:12,940
that that language service the BBC was doing

308
00:18:12,940 --> 00:18:14,860
were simply update stories.

309
00:18:14,860 --> 00:18:18,860
This happened, or here's an update on some ongoing story,

310
00:18:18,860 --> 00:18:22,500
which is most of what we traditionally do in journalism.

311
00:18:22,500 --> 00:18:24,820
But again, the idea here was to figure out

312
00:18:24,820 --> 00:18:28,800
what BBC readers and listeners actually needed.

313
00:18:28,800 --> 00:18:31,600
the kinds of stories people would turn to them for.

314
00:18:31,600 --> 00:18:33,780
So update me is one of the labels

315
00:18:33,780 --> 00:18:35,960
and most of their stories got that,

316
00:18:35,960 --> 00:18:38,580
but there were other kinds, like connect me,

317
00:18:38,580 --> 00:18:41,180
stories that helped people get involved.

318
00:18:41,180 --> 00:18:43,820
Keep me engaged, stories about the stuff

319
00:18:43,820 --> 00:18:45,520
people are talking about right now

320
00:18:45,520 --> 00:18:48,580
and how you can be a part of that conversation.

321
00:18:48,580 --> 00:18:53,580
Even divert me, stories that were just funny or entertaining.

322
00:18:53,720 --> 00:18:56,580
And once the BBC labeled their stories

323
00:18:56,580 --> 00:18:58,100
and paid attention to the stuff

324
00:18:58,100 --> 00:19:00,780
people were reading and listening to,

325
00:19:00,780 --> 00:19:02,580
it turned out that the connection

326
00:19:02,580 --> 00:19:05,040
and engagement and entertainment stories

327
00:19:05,040 --> 00:19:07,480
were way more popular.

328
00:19:07,480 --> 00:19:12,480
Update stories, aka most of their stories, not so much.

329
00:19:13,140 --> 00:19:17,860
So they pivoted, and that meant publishing less stuff.

330
00:19:17,860 --> 00:19:22,460
But more of the stuff people actually wanted.

331
00:19:22,460 --> 00:19:24,620
And their audience tripled.

332
00:19:24,620 --> 00:19:29,600
Now how is it you could do fewer pieces and triple your audience?

333
00:19:29,600 --> 00:19:34,360
It's because you're doing more stories that took more time, that were more useful, more

334
00:19:34,360 --> 00:19:35,360
valuable.

335
00:19:35,360 --> 00:19:41,200
They weren't these ephemeral, this just happened stories, which tend to get very low engagement.

336
00:19:41,200 --> 00:19:48,600
Nick, I think a lot about this time I was preparing to vote in my hometown years ago.

337
00:19:48,600 --> 00:19:51,960
And you know, at that time I could have told you about dozens of national issues and how

338
00:19:51,960 --> 00:19:56,840
they were developing, like what this federal candidate or that executive branch official

339
00:19:56,840 --> 00:20:01,360
had done that week, the latest development and some international standoff, etc. But

340
00:20:01,360 --> 00:20:07,520
I could not for the life of me find substantial information about the local candidates I was

341
00:20:07,520 --> 00:20:12,360
supposed to be voting for. My point is my brain was full of all of this national and

342
00:20:12,360 --> 00:20:17,840
international stuff and I could get all of the updates on that. But what I needed and

343
00:20:17,840 --> 00:20:19,880
And what I wanted?

344
00:20:19,880 --> 00:20:21,840
That was hard to come by.

345
00:20:21,840 --> 00:20:25,000
You wanted the lowercase d democracy stuff.

346
00:20:25,000 --> 00:20:26,120
Little lowercase d.

347
00:20:26,120 --> 00:20:30,600
The democracy closest to me, the one I had a real chance of affecting.

348
00:20:30,600 --> 00:20:34,920
With my vote, my participation, my opinions, my ideas.

349
00:20:34,920 --> 00:20:39,320
Instead, I had this, who are you people and what do you want?

350
00:20:39,320 --> 00:20:40,320
Feeling.

351
00:20:40,320 --> 00:20:46,720
Also, I was a journalist at that point and I was feeling, oh no, am I a part of my own

352
00:20:46,720 --> 00:20:51,940
problem? Or at the very least not a part of your own solution. Oh yeah that's good.

353
00:21:02,200 --> 00:21:06,300
All right Nick we have talked about stories with a job to be done about the

354
00:21:06,300 --> 00:21:11,540
kinds of things it seems like people need but the question remains how can

355
00:21:11,540 --> 00:21:19,540
today's journalists get there, provide that, serve that lowercase d democracy.

356
00:21:19,540 --> 00:21:21,820
And I'm going to get to that right after a quick break.

357
00:21:41,540 --> 00:21:43,620
for anyone on your list.

358
00:21:43,620 --> 00:21:45,940
Now that your holiday shopping list is figured out,

359
00:21:45,940 --> 00:21:48,060
enjoy this bird singing Jingle Bells.

360
00:21:52,780 --> 00:21:55,280
Give the gift of scratchers from the California Lottery.

361
00:21:55,280 --> 00:21:57,360
A little play can make your day.

362
00:21:57,360 --> 00:21:58,460
Please play responsibly.

363
00:21:58,460 --> 00:22:00,980
Must be 18 years or older to purchase, play or claim.

364
00:22:02,860 --> 00:22:03,700
We're back.

365
00:22:03,700 --> 00:22:05,300
We're talking with Tom Rosenstiel,

366
00:22:05,300 --> 00:22:07,900
author of the next journalism.

367
00:22:07,900 --> 00:22:09,900
And Nick, we're trying to figure out

368
00:22:09,900 --> 00:22:14,900
why the free press is perhaps not doing what it says it does,

369
00:22:15,120 --> 00:22:18,120
being essential to democracy.

370
00:22:18,120 --> 00:22:19,920
So we talked about being of use to people

371
00:22:19,920 --> 00:22:23,040
by paying attention to what people actually need,

372
00:22:23,040 --> 00:22:24,480
but there's another problem

373
00:22:24,480 --> 00:22:26,640
that Tom thinks we're going to need to address

374
00:22:26,640 --> 00:22:30,360
if the press is going to better serve democracy.

375
00:22:31,240 --> 00:22:34,000
A lot of people just do not trust the press.

376
00:22:34,000 --> 00:22:36,320
So there's lots of research now about trust

377
00:22:36,320 --> 00:22:38,280
because the numbers have been looking bad

378
00:22:38,280 --> 00:22:39,680
for a couple of decades.

379
00:22:39,680 --> 00:22:42,640
And so a bunch of scholars have delved into this.

380
00:22:42,640 --> 00:22:46,120
And the answer to declining trust has been,

381
00:22:46,120 --> 00:22:47,600
well, let's become more transparent

382
00:22:47,600 --> 00:22:49,160
about how we do our work.

383
00:22:49,160 --> 00:22:51,320
And if we show people how we do our work,

384
00:22:51,320 --> 00:22:53,560
they'll trust us, because they'll see what we're very

385
00:22:53,560 --> 00:22:55,760
competent, we're very experienced.

386
00:22:55,760 --> 00:22:57,920
We check our facts and things like that.

387
00:22:57,920 --> 00:23:00,560
What the research shows is that the kind of trust

388
00:23:00,560 --> 00:23:03,360
that is required for a news organization is what they

389
00:23:03,360 --> 00:23:05,040
call relational trust.

390
00:23:05,040 --> 00:23:07,800
So I have a feeling this is gonna mean more than just

391
00:23:07,800 --> 00:23:09,960
telling people they can trust us.

392
00:23:09,960 --> 00:23:13,520
Which is another thing that we journalists say all the time.

393
00:23:13,520 --> 00:23:18,520
We do trustworthy work, but labeling it as trustworthy

394
00:23:18,600 --> 00:23:23,200
is not the same thing as earning the trust of people.

395
00:23:23,200 --> 00:23:27,080
And that relational trust includes three key things,

396
00:23:27,080 --> 00:23:30,060
only two of which I think journalists really think about.

397
00:23:30,060 --> 00:23:32,760
One is, are you competent?

398
00:23:32,760 --> 00:23:35,160
And that's, we think we're really good at that.

399
00:23:35,160 --> 00:23:39,300
And if we could just explain why, how we do what we do, people should trust us.

400
00:23:39,880 --> 00:23:41,560
Another is, are we accountable?

401
00:23:41,560 --> 00:23:43,640
In other words, if we screw up, will we admit it?

402
00:23:44,200 --> 00:23:50,560
There were grudgingly coming along, but we don't like big corrections and things like that.

403
00:23:51,000 --> 00:23:54,840
But the third one is one that most journalists do not even think about.

404
00:23:54,840 --> 00:23:59,160
And that is benevolence, which it really means, what is your motive?

405
00:23:59,400 --> 00:24:02,640
Can I trust that you are in it for the right reasons?

406
00:24:02,640 --> 00:24:07,400
And when you dive into the distrust or the trust numbers,

407
00:24:07,400 --> 00:24:11,200
it's that question of what do I think your motive is,

408
00:24:11,200 --> 00:24:13,080
which is the real sore spot.

409
00:24:13,080 --> 00:24:17,480
People think we write stories to sell newspapers,

410
00:24:17,480 --> 00:24:22,000
you know, to get clicks that we are secretly working

411
00:24:22,000 --> 00:24:25,680
for the Democratic Party, or that we, if you're on the left,

412
00:24:25,680 --> 00:24:27,880
that you are a voice of the establishment

413
00:24:27,880 --> 00:24:32,040
and probably a white middle-aged male establishment.

414
00:24:32,040 --> 00:24:36,840
There's a lot of inferring that goes on into motive.

415
00:24:36,840 --> 00:24:40,340
Now, what can we do about that is a challenging thing.

416
00:24:43,200 --> 00:24:46,920
You know, Hannah, if someone rarely admits they're wrong

417
00:24:46,920 --> 00:24:49,320
and spends their days talking about what is wrong

418
00:24:49,320 --> 00:24:51,820
with other people in the rest of the world,

419
00:24:51,820 --> 00:24:55,480
I gotta say that for me, that does not inspire confidence.

420
00:24:55,480 --> 00:24:56,440
Same here.

421
00:24:56,440 --> 00:24:58,120
Easy to see that speck of sawdust

422
00:24:58,120 --> 00:25:01,560
and someone else's eye not so much the plank in your own.

423
00:25:01,560 --> 00:25:04,200
When Tom talks about suspicions about journalists,

424
00:25:04,200 --> 00:25:08,000
this lack of trust, he explained that there is a disconnect

425
00:25:08,000 --> 00:25:09,880
between what journalists value

426
00:25:09,880 --> 00:25:12,180
and what the public values.

427
00:25:12,180 --> 00:25:14,520
You know, we have these certain values,

428
00:25:14,520 --> 00:25:17,400
like people in power, you know, can't be trusted.

429
00:25:17,400 --> 00:25:18,920
We have to watch them.

430
00:25:18,920 --> 00:25:22,080
We make society better by spotlighting its problems.

431
00:25:22,080 --> 00:25:23,640
The more facts you have,

432
00:25:23,640 --> 00:25:25,640
if you have enough facts, you'll have the truth.

433
00:25:25,640 --> 00:25:27,400
People with less power in society,

434
00:25:27,400 --> 00:25:29,160
we need to raise their voices up.

435
00:25:29,160 --> 00:25:33,640
These are not bad values, but there are limits to them.

436
00:25:33,640 --> 00:25:39,000
So the press is the watchdog, as the one holding power to account, finding out the bad stuff

437
00:25:39,000 --> 00:25:40,640
that's going on.

438
00:25:40,640 --> 00:25:44,040
That seems like it must be supporting democracy, right?

439
00:25:44,040 --> 00:25:48,440
You're rooting out corruption, making sure our institutions work.

440
00:25:48,440 --> 00:25:51,080
That is making the world better, isn't it?

441
00:25:51,080 --> 00:25:58,400
So one of the things that breeds suspicions about our motives when we test this is, we

442
00:25:58,400 --> 00:26:02,400
We took five core values that journalists share,

443
00:26:02,400 --> 00:26:04,200
some of which I just articulated,

444
00:26:04,200 --> 00:26:07,320
and tested how much support was there for them.

445
00:26:07,320 --> 00:26:10,840
The only one that had majority support was

446
00:26:10,840 --> 00:26:12,800
the more facts you have about something,

447
00:26:12,800 --> 00:26:16,400
the more likely you are to understand it accurately.

448
00:26:17,740 --> 00:26:20,960
Very few people thought you make society better,

449
00:26:20,960 --> 00:26:24,160
like only 20% of people thought you made society better

450
00:26:24,160 --> 00:26:26,160
by spotlighting its problems.

451
00:26:26,160 --> 00:26:30,980
They're more inclined to think, yes, but, yes, I want to know the problem, but that's

452
00:26:30,980 --> 00:26:34,040
not really the only way to make society better.

453
00:26:34,040 --> 00:26:41,740
So we think we're just doing our job and that people should understand this when in reality,

454
00:26:41,740 --> 00:26:48,280
what we think about our job, people are inherently suspicious of the way we want to go about it.

455
00:26:48,280 --> 00:26:53,700
To be clear, Tom is not saying that journalists should stop publishing stories on corruption,

456
00:26:53,700 --> 00:26:56,400
should stop holding power to account.

457
00:26:56,400 --> 00:26:58,680
Some people might overreact to the approach

458
00:26:58,680 --> 00:26:59,960
that I'm advocating and say,

459
00:26:59,960 --> 00:27:01,000
well, you know, are you saying

460
00:27:01,000 --> 00:27:02,560
that we shouldn't do investigative reporting?

461
00:27:02,560 --> 00:27:03,400
No.

462
00:27:03,400 --> 00:27:04,960
But as Tom puts it,

463
00:27:04,960 --> 00:27:07,560
journalists often think they're doing more investigating

464
00:27:07,560 --> 00:27:09,440
than they're actually doing.

465
00:27:09,440 --> 00:27:13,600
He is calling for, you know, asking the right questions,

466
00:27:13,600 --> 00:27:17,640
asking whether or not someone is doing a good job.

467
00:27:17,640 --> 00:27:19,280
And then deeper than that,

468
00:27:19,280 --> 00:27:23,000
trying to understand what a good job would actually mean.

469
00:27:23,000 --> 00:27:25,800
When you start to ask those questions, you start to dig deeper.

470
00:27:26,280 --> 00:27:30,960
And those are the ways that you might find that corruption, but along the way,

471
00:27:30,960 --> 00:27:36,000
you are able to provide your audience with more meaningful, more useful

472
00:27:36,040 --> 00:27:38,400
coverage of those people in power.

473
00:27:38,840 --> 00:27:43,280
All of this makes sense to me, Hannah, but isn't there another reason people

474
00:27:43,280 --> 00:27:45,120
are suspicious of the press?

475
00:27:45,760 --> 00:27:49,480
Because they have been told to be suspicious of the press?

476
00:27:49,600 --> 00:27:50,440
That's part of it.

477
00:27:50,440 --> 00:27:56,040
But according to Tom, so is the way a lot of journalists go about their work.

478
00:27:56,040 --> 00:28:00,440
We don't quite get that there's some skepticism about our motives.

479
00:28:00,440 --> 00:28:14,040
So this ideology we have, it's not really a partisan one, but it has political implications for people.

480
00:28:14,040 --> 00:28:20,040
It kind of leans, it's a little anti-authoritarian, it's a little, you know, suspicious.

481
00:28:20,040 --> 00:28:22,360
It's a little negative.

482
00:28:22,360 --> 00:28:24,680
It didn't used to be such a problem

483
00:28:24,680 --> 00:28:26,760
when there were three TV channels

484
00:28:26,760 --> 00:28:28,840
and you had to get all your national news from them.

485
00:28:28,840 --> 00:28:30,960
There was one newspaper in every town,

486
00:28:30,960 --> 00:28:32,600
three local TV stations.

487
00:28:32,600 --> 00:28:34,600
I didn't have a lot of choice.

488
00:28:34,600 --> 00:28:37,520
The decline in trust correlates very, very strongly

489
00:28:37,520 --> 00:28:39,320
to people having more choices

490
00:28:39,320 --> 00:28:42,080
and then hearing more stuff from other people,

491
00:28:42,080 --> 00:28:45,400
many of which decide that they're gonna acquire

492
00:28:45,400 --> 00:28:48,840
an audience base by criticizing the mainstream media.

493
00:28:50,040 --> 00:29:00,040
So what can we do? One of the things that I advocate is we really need to understand

494
00:29:00,040 --> 00:29:06,040
the audience better. One thing Tom says journalists can do, and this is how I kind of take it,

495
00:29:06,040 --> 00:29:12,840
is remember that you are also a human being in the world. You also need things. There

496
00:29:12,840 --> 00:29:19,280
are these ways that you can discover the bubble that you've slipped into as a journalist.

497
00:29:19,280 --> 00:29:24,680
One of them is a simple exercise called the dinner table exercise where you get journalists

498
00:29:24,680 --> 00:29:27,280
in a room and you say, okay, you're at a dinner party.

499
00:29:27,280 --> 00:29:29,560
Let's say you've got kids.

500
00:29:29,560 --> 00:29:34,800
Just tell stories about your engagement with the school system in the last year.

501
00:29:34,800 --> 00:29:39,040
And typically what happens in this exercise, people say, oh, the buses are constantly

502
00:29:39,040 --> 00:29:43,880
breaking down or, you know, I don't understand why the school has to start so early.

503
00:29:43,880 --> 00:29:44,880
My kids are asleep.

504
00:29:44,880 --> 00:29:46,840
I have to wake them up.

505
00:29:46,840 --> 00:29:50,980
You know, could they start at a normal time, a time that's better for them?

506
00:29:50,980 --> 00:29:54,140
And then you stop and you say, okay, make a story list.

507
00:29:54,140 --> 00:29:58,860
And the story list that comes out of the organic stories that people tell about their personal

508
00:29:58,860 --> 00:30:04,900
interaction are drastically different than if you'd said, okay, take a half an hour and

509
00:30:04,900 --> 00:30:06,140
make a story list.

510
00:30:06,140 --> 00:30:09,420
Hannah, this happens to us a lot.

511
00:30:09,420 --> 00:30:12,700
For example, when you had to register your car in New Hampshire.

512
00:30:12,700 --> 00:30:15,100
Yeah, you think you have the right documents,

513
00:30:15,100 --> 00:30:17,860
you think you are in the right place, but you are wrong.

514
00:30:17,860 --> 00:30:20,060
And as you were ranting about it,

515
00:30:20,060 --> 00:30:22,780
you were like, now this is a Civics 101 episode.

516
00:30:22,780 --> 00:30:24,540
Because I am also a person in the world

517
00:30:24,540 --> 00:30:27,280
and I could really use a detailed explanation

518
00:30:27,280 --> 00:30:30,380
as to how to help make my life better

519
00:30:30,380 --> 00:30:32,400
when it comes to this situation.

520
00:30:32,400 --> 00:30:34,740
Which reminds me, I need to make a Civics 101 episode

521
00:30:34,740 --> 00:30:37,060
about navigating health insurance because,

522
00:30:38,020 --> 00:30:41,540
oh my, am I trying to navigate that right now?

523
00:30:41,540 --> 00:30:43,940
Okay, talk about healthcare, like, you know,

524
00:30:43,940 --> 00:30:46,260
or talk about government, you know, and I'm, yeah, man,

525
00:30:46,260 --> 00:30:47,620
why don't you get my car registered

526
00:30:47,620 --> 00:30:49,300
and that tip forever, whatever.

527
00:30:49,300 --> 00:30:52,140
So that's one way and very easy way.

528
00:30:52,140 --> 00:30:53,820
And actually when you do that,

529
00:30:53,820 --> 00:30:57,420
it can be very effective to have nonjournalists in the group,

530
00:30:57,420 --> 00:30:59,740
but also then go talk to people, you know,

531
00:30:59,740 --> 00:31:03,580
publications do events and those events are a great way

532
00:31:03,580 --> 00:31:05,380
to just get with people.

533
00:31:05,380 --> 00:31:09,340
And all of this is, I mean, it's not like journalism

534
00:31:09,340 --> 00:31:11,500
is frozen in time.

535
00:31:11,500 --> 00:31:14,900
But there was a time when we were fat and happy.

536
00:31:17,460 --> 00:31:18,940
I mean, I worked at the LA Times.

537
00:31:18,940 --> 00:31:21,820
We made so much money, we didn't know what to do with it.

538
00:31:21,820 --> 00:31:24,360
The lunch room or the Picasso room,

539
00:31:24,360 --> 00:31:27,700
because there were Picasso's hanging on the wall.

540
00:31:27,700 --> 00:31:28,780
Oh, get out.

541
00:31:28,780 --> 00:31:30,540
I'm not kidding.

542
00:31:30,540 --> 00:31:32,740
Not the plebe lunch room,

543
00:31:32,740 --> 00:31:36,620
but the lunch room for editors and more senior staff.

544
00:31:36,620 --> 00:31:39,900
And those days are gone.

545
00:31:39,900 --> 00:31:43,620
But in those days, we didn't really want to interact

546
00:31:43,620 --> 00:31:46,380
with the great unwashed public that read us.

547
00:31:46,380 --> 00:31:48,780
There was an editor in Oregon who said,

548
00:31:48,780 --> 00:31:51,900
the attitude in the newsroom is so derisive

549
00:31:51,900 --> 00:31:54,740
towards the public that she instituted a policy

550
00:31:54,740 --> 00:31:57,860
where each day a different reporter in the newsroom

551
00:31:57,860 --> 00:32:00,700
was gonna have to answer the phone from the public.

552
00:32:00,700 --> 00:32:02,340
And she said, there were a couple of reporters,

553
00:32:02,340 --> 00:32:05,700
they thought they were so grumpy

554
00:32:05,700 --> 00:32:08,320
that they might actually exempt them from this,

555
00:32:08,320 --> 00:32:12,300
but they just wanted people to like have some interaction

556
00:32:12,300 --> 00:32:13,260
with the public.

557
00:32:13,260 --> 00:32:17,180
We've come a long way, but we need to go further.

558
00:32:17,180 --> 00:32:18,660
And the reason we need to go further

559
00:32:18,660 --> 00:32:21,460
is the business model now requires it.

560
00:32:21,460 --> 00:32:26,460
It's such a shift to think that our economic viability

561
00:32:27,180 --> 00:32:30,220
depends on people paying for the product.

562
00:32:30,220 --> 00:32:33,940
That is just, it's a tectonic shift

563
00:32:33,940 --> 00:32:37,380
in the way that this profession needs to survive.

564
00:32:40,980 --> 00:32:45,980
So Hannah, it should be obvious how absurd it is

565
00:32:45,980 --> 00:32:47,500
that a newsroom of journalists

566
00:32:47,500 --> 00:32:50,060
did not want to speak to the public.

567
00:32:50,060 --> 00:32:54,260
But I think understanding the decline in newspapers

568
00:32:54,260 --> 00:32:56,380
and radio and even television news,

569
00:32:56,380 --> 00:32:59,480
understanding how we went from Picasso's on the wall

570
00:32:59,480 --> 00:33:02,720
to whatever the opposite of Picasso's on the wall is,

571
00:33:02,720 --> 00:33:07,720
does come down to understanding, like really understanding

572
00:33:08,680 --> 00:33:11,540
that we need to talk to the public.

573
00:33:11,540 --> 00:33:14,080
Yeah, and actually that brings me to the last question

574
00:33:14,080 --> 00:33:15,860
that I had for Tom.

575
00:33:15,860 --> 00:33:18,720
And you know this, Nick, it's something that I ask everybody

576
00:33:18,720 --> 00:33:21,760
at the end of an interview because I almost always miss

577
00:33:21,760 --> 00:33:22,720
something.

578
00:33:22,720 --> 00:33:24,960
Is there anything that we didn't touch on

579
00:33:24,960 --> 00:33:29,960
that you would like, either journalists or nonjournalists,

580
00:33:29,960 --> 00:33:32,800
other members of the public who listen to this podcast

581
00:33:32,800 --> 00:33:35,840
to understand about either this book

582
00:33:35,840 --> 00:33:37,920
or the way that you are looking

583
00:33:37,920 --> 00:33:39,440
at the media landscape today.

584
00:33:39,440 --> 00:33:42,200
Yeah, there is one thing that's important,

585
00:33:42,200 --> 00:33:43,040
I think really important,

586
00:33:43,040 --> 00:33:46,400
and that is the question of do we have any common facts anymore

587
00:33:46,400 --> 00:33:48,800
or do we live in our own separate little silos?

588
00:33:48,800 --> 00:33:53,760
Fact silos.

589
00:33:53,760 --> 00:33:56,520
The reality is not quite as dire as it seems

590
00:33:56,520 --> 00:33:58,480
when you just think about politics.

591
00:33:58,480 --> 00:34:02,040
There's plenty of common facts about our sports teams,

592
00:34:02,040 --> 00:34:05,340
our kids' schools, the roads, the weather.

593
00:34:05,340 --> 00:34:07,360
People live in a mixed media environment

594
00:34:07,360 --> 00:34:08,640
a lot more than we think they do.

595
00:34:08,640 --> 00:34:10,760
Nobody actually is just getting everything

596
00:34:10,760 --> 00:34:13,160
from Fox News or MSNBC.

597
00:34:13,160 --> 00:34:16,440
When I say nobody, the vast majority

598
00:34:16,440 --> 00:34:18,200
live in a mixed media environment.

599
00:34:18,200 --> 00:34:23,200
But journalists really need to see it

600
00:34:23,220 --> 00:34:26,760
as part of their mission to create a public square,

601
00:34:26,760 --> 00:34:31,480
to create a place where we can understand each other,

602
00:34:31,480 --> 00:34:35,120
but that we can make people who disagree with each other

603
00:34:35,120 --> 00:34:37,800
comprehensible to each other.

604
00:34:37,800 --> 00:34:41,280
If on election day or after election day,

605
00:34:41,280 --> 00:34:44,160
the result is incomprehensible,

606
00:34:44,160 --> 00:34:46,320
that's actually a failure of journalism.

607
00:34:46,320 --> 00:34:48,080
The problem isn't that the audience

608
00:34:48,080 --> 00:34:50,440
doesn't understand our version of truth,

609
00:34:50,440 --> 00:34:53,120
the problem is we don't understand the audience.

610
00:34:53,120 --> 00:35:06,440
We need to make the country, however challenging or polarized it might be, we need to make

611
00:35:06,440 --> 00:35:11,000
the country more understandable to the people in it.

612
00:35:11,000 --> 00:35:17,840
At the moment, a lot of journalism enables polarization because it's a way of getting

613
00:35:17,840 --> 00:35:21,960
attention show, like look at this outrageous thing this guy did.

614
00:35:21,960 --> 00:35:26,640
We're actually rewarding that guy by spotlighting his outrage.

615
00:35:26,640 --> 00:35:32,980
What I say in the book is, instead of thinking that our job is to alert a passive public

616
00:35:32,980 --> 00:35:38,800
to things they should know, we should define our mission as helping people live their lives

617
00:35:38,800 --> 00:35:40,680
and improve their communities.

618
00:35:40,680 --> 00:35:49,640
And to that end, Nick, you know how we so often say in the Civics 101 credits, if you

619
00:35:49,640 --> 00:35:52,920
If you have a question for Civics 101, you can submit that at our website.

620
00:35:52,920 --> 00:35:55,000
I have said that more times than I can count.

621
00:35:55,000 --> 00:35:57,920
And how often do you listen to the credits of a podcast?

622
00:35:57,920 --> 00:35:59,640
I plead the fifth, Hannah.

623
00:35:59,640 --> 00:36:01,520
Speaking of which, we should do an episode on that.

624
00:36:01,520 --> 00:36:03,520
How about doing an episode on what the people want?

625
00:36:03,520 --> 00:36:04,520
They might want that.

626
00:36:04,520 --> 00:36:06,520
You don't know until you ask, do you?

627
00:36:06,520 --> 00:36:10,280
So do you guys want an episode on what pleading the fifth means?

628
00:36:10,280 --> 00:36:14,700
And while we're at it, what is something in your neighborhood that makes absolutely zero

629
00:36:14,700 --> 00:36:19,900
sense to you or something that really helped a problem in your town, your school district,

630
00:36:19,900 --> 00:36:25,300
on your highways with your trash pickup.

